document.write('<p>For nearly 30 years, the Sundance Film Festival has been a champion of independent filmmaking. This year, there was a record representation of black films. Julie Walker of <em>The Root</em> takes a look at some of the featured movies and argues that, unlike the recent Oscar nominations, diversity is flourishing at the Sundance Film Festival.</p><p><a href=\"http://www.npr.org/templates/email/emailAFriend.php?storyId=133428188\">» E-Mail This</a>     <a href=\"http://del.icio.us/post?url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.npr.org%2Ftemplates%2Fstory%2Fstory.php%3FstoryId%3D133428188\">» Add to Del.icio.us</a></p>');
